From d389b4bb1c2e4bc90f8b9ec6df1dec5b051c9538 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: elliotttate <elliotttate@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 12:57:05 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 108/109] Aurora: 120 FPS in-between frames, on a steady
 present clock

aurora_set_frame_interp_steps(3) (DOL_AURORA_FRAME_INTERP_STEPS=3): three
in-between frames each game frame, at a quarter, half and three quarters of
the way, for a 120 Hz display. A matched draw is blended once per step: the
camera's part motion is the screw motion's power at t (exactly half at 0.5,
so four quarter steps make the whole), the helper thread stages a block and
a particle's vertices per step, and the in-between frame being encoded reads
its step's. frame_interp_test covers even steps for a camera that turns and
moves, a moving draw and a particle.

Presents are held aside and shown on one clock: an in-between frame every
quarter (or half) of a game frame, continuing from the frame before's last,
from two alternating sets of held frames so a new game frame never forces
the one before's out early, with what is due presented between replays and
every 512 commands of a pass. Presenting the first in-between frame at once
and the rest after it put them in bursts at 120 Hz (the sea shimmered), and
60 Hz alternated 22 and 12 ms between presents; now 8.4 and 16.5 ms apart.
DOL_AURORA_PRESENT_CLOCK=0 keeps the old 60 Hz timing; DOL_AURORA_PRESENT_LOG=1
logs each present.

The in-between uniform area has 32 MB per step: at 120 Hz a beach needed
about 55 MB, the blocks past 32 did not fit, and those draws kept the next
frame's transforms in two of the three in-between frames (the sea blinked
out on the shore).
